fact WorkItem(id: "WI-721", description: some(value: "Associated (dispatched) relations: per-instance rule clauses as spec members (proposal 052 Future direction + 044). A spec may declare an ASSOCIATED relation -- a rule head with NO clauses (a relational signature, like an abstract operation); each PROVIDER of the spec supplies its own clauses; generic code that 'requires' the spec cites the relation and it dispatches to the DISCHARGED instance's clauses. The relational dual of a spec operation. Ref: docs/proposals/052-rules-as-stream-valued-operations.md 'Future direction -- associated (dispatched) relations' + 044. CORRECTED SCOPE (load-bearing framing -- do not re-derive the old wrong premise): this is NOT a name-visibility feature. 'requires' is SORT COMPOSITION and ALREADY exposes a spec's rules bare, UNIFORM with its operations, subject to the entity-variant-exposure filter (an entity-bearing required sort leaks only its constructors, hiding ops AND rules; an entity-LESS spec leaks everything) -- verified; there is NO ops/rules asymmetry. The genuine gap is PER-INSTANCE DISPATCH of rule clauses; name resolution / loader is UNTOUCHED. TWO IMPLEMENTATION PIECES: (1) PROVIDER-SCOPED CLAUSES -- a provider's 'rule edge ...' associated with ITS provision, not the global rules_by_functor / rules_by_label index every rule lands in today; the spec's own 'edge' is a clause-less signature. (2) REQUIREMENT-DIRECTED CLAUSE SELECTION (the load-bearing engine capability) -- bare 'edge' in a 'requires Graph' clause fires the DISCHARGED Graph instance's clauses, read from the resolver's local-interpretation env (the SLD analog of a frame's requirements, already threaded for rule-body requirements by WI-300 / find_dictionary). This is the clause-level parallel of WI-222 defer-to-requirement (which does exactly this for operation IMPLS). COHERENCE: (a) an associated rule = a clause-less spec head; a spec's ordinary clause-bearing rule stays STATIC (one shared relation, composed in unchanged) -- distinguished by the spec head having clauses or not, NOT a new visibility rule; (b) AMBIGUITY = LOAD ERROR (two required specs declaring the same associated-rule name -> reject at load, qualify to disambiguate; decidable statically at the requires site, so loud -- unlike a plain unqualified rule miss which is a silent 0-solutions); (c) a sort's OWN rule of that name OVERRIDES the associated one (mirror the operation override policy, WI-444 / WI-411). OUT OF SCOPE: any name-resolution / loader visibility change (already works via requires composition); the Relation[T] VALUE surface (that is WI-714, orthogonal -- this feature is SUBGOAL-position dispatch of an associated relation cited in a rule body, not relations-as-values). DEPENDS on delivered machinery it reuses/parallels: WI-300 (rule-body requirement dictionaries / find_dictionary -- the local-interpretation channel it rides) and WI-222 (defer-to-requirement -- the operation-dispatch parallel). Independent of WI-714. ACCEPTANCE (the Graph/edge/reachable example runs PER-INSTANCE): spec Graph declares clause-less 'rule edge(?a, ?b)'; sort SocialNet provides Graph with 'rule edge(?a,?b) :- follows(?a,?b)'; sort RoadMap provides Graph with 'rule edge(?a,?b) :- road(from:?a, to:?b)'; generic 'sort Reach requires Graph' with 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?b)' + 'rule reachable(?a,?b) :- edge(?a,?c), reachable(?c,?b)'; then reachable resolved against a SocialNet instance yields follows-transitive-reachability and against a RoadMap instance yields road-transitive-reachability -- DISTINCT per instance (the crux); PLUS cross-spec ambiguity (two required specs both declaring 'edge') is a loud LOAD error; a sort's local 'edge' overrides the associated one; cargo-test green via rustland/scripts/test.sh with tests per piece. Rust-only (loader storage + resolver dispatch; scaland has no operation loading)."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-300", "WI-222"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: PreOpened)
